2.7 Change, rebuild, observe
If only the string in main.c changes, we can predict the consequences before rebuilding:

main.o must change;
start.o does not;
the final ELF must be linked again;
linker.ld does not change;
QEMU itself does not change.

These are the logical dependencies. A concrete Makefile may encode them at a coarser granularity and therefore rebuild more than the minimum set of intermediate files.
This small exercise is about maintaining a causal model of the system:

predict → change → build → run → observe → explain

That pattern will be reused when the changes are no longer only strings, but operating-system mechanisms.

2.9 Yield, exit, resume, and restart
Yield and exit both transfer control back to the operating system, but they have different meanings.

yield: the execution is not finished and must be able to continue later;
exit: the execution is finished and must never continue.

The distinction becomes visible in this intended interface:
uart_puts("A\n");
process_yield();
uart_puts("B\n");
process_exit();
process_yield() and process_exit() are not library functions we already have. They are functions our minimal operating system still has to implement.
After process_yield(), the operating system has control. When the process runs again, it must continue with uart_puts("B\n"). Printing A again would mean that we restarted the execution instead of resuming it.
This is the conceptual step that makes the next lecture necessary.

struct process p;
struct process *q = &p;



p.entry = worker;
p.entry();

3.2 Building the process object
We first give the execution a concrete representation. The following struct fragments add one part at a time; the complete definition follows after we establish what must be saved in a context.

3.2.1 Entry function
An entry function is where a new process begins its work. For example:
void worker(void) { /* process-specific work */ }
The process must remember which function to enter. Its first field is therefore a function pointer:
struct process {
    void (*entry)(void);
};
Read void (*entry)(void) as: entry is a pointer to a function taking no arguments and returning void. Repeating that declaration is awkward, so a typedef gives the pointer type a name:
typedef void (*process_entry_t)(void);
The field can now be written process_entry_t entry;. This names the same function-pointer type; it does not declare or call an entry function.


3.2.2 Owned stack storage
The process also reserves memory for its own call stack:
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

#define STACK_SIZE 4096

struct process {
    process_entry_t entry;
    uint8_t stack[STACK_SIZE];
};
uint8_t, defined by &lt;stdint.h&gt;, is an unsigned 8-bit integer type. A char stack[STACK_SIZE] array would also be a viable choice for raw byte storage in our RV64 environment: a character array need not contain text. We use uint8_t to make the byte-storage role explicit and keep our treatment of integer types focused on &lt;stdint.h&gt;. This header is available in our freestanding RISC-V toolchain; including it does not require linking a C library.
At this point, treat the array simply as memory owned by the process; we will explain its use below.


3.2.3 Lifecycle information
The manager needs to know whether the process may run and, once it finishes, which exit status it produced:
enum process_state { READY, RUNNING, FINISHED };

struct process {
    process_entry_t entry;
    uint8_t stack[STACK_SIZE];
    enum process_state state;
    int exit_status;
};
An enum supplies named alternatives for the state. READY means the process may run, RUNNING means it is executing, and FINISHED means it must not run again. exit_status records the result once it finishes.


3.2.4 What is still missing?
We can now remember where to start, where call data can live, whether the process may run, and how it finished. But suppose execution pauses here:
helper();
process_yield();
/* continue here later */
These fields do not yet tell us how to continue at exactly that point. We need to understand both the stack’s runtime data and the CPU register values that make that data usable.

3.6 The complete process object
The saved context supplies the missing part. Together, entry, stack, context, and lifecycle information represent our managed execution.
The public representation used by Lecture 3, Exercise 2, and Checkpoint 1 is:
struct process {
    process_entry_t entry;
    uint8_t stack[STACK_SIZE];
    struct context context;
    enum process_state state;
    int exit_status;
};
The five fields have distinct roles:

entry: where a new process begins;
stack: process-owned raw byte storage for its call stack;
context: saved CPU register state needed to continue execution;
state: whether the process is READY, RUNNING, or FINISHED;
exit_status: the result recorded when the process finishes.

Exercise 2 uses this exact field order because its precompiled process library was built against this representation. Checkpoint 1 keeps the same interface and representation while students provide the implementation.

3.8 Implementation boundary
The course provides the machinery whose complexity is incidental to the CP1 competence:

existing startup and linker support;
UART output already available in the starter;
the context representation;
the RV64 context-switch assembly boundary.

Students construct the process abstraction and lifecycle in C:

process representation;
owned stack;
first-run context;
entry handling;
run;
yield;
exit;
observable resume behaviour.

CP1 does not require timer interrupts, preemption, several runnable processes, a scheduler, privilege separation, virtual memory, dynamic loading, or dynamic allocation.

3.9 Public process interface
The first-process abstraction uses this public C interface:
void process_create(struct process *process,
                    process_entry_t entry);
void process_run(struct process *process);
void process_yield(void);
void process_exit(int status);
Exercise 2 links this interface against a provided implementation. Checkpoint 1 keeps the same interface but replaces that implementation with student-written C code.

3.11 The complete CP1 observation
Exercise 2 and Checkpoint 1 use the same small permutation program. Its observable behaviour is:
process: before yield: VBS1
os: process yielded
process: result: VB1S
os: process exited
The interesting evidence is not the strings themselves. The output demonstrates that:

the process starts using its own execution context;
worker() calls permute(), which modifies process-local data and yields before returning;
the operating system regains control while that call chain remains suspended;
the same permute() invocation later continues after process_yield();
the local array progresses from VBS1 to VB1S, showing that the same execution state survived;
the process exits with status 0 and becomes FINISHED;
the operating system regains control again and must not resume the finished process.

4.3 One managed execution can contain several active calls
Exercise 2 uses this entry function:
static void worker(void)
{
    char value[] = "BVS1";
    permute(value);
    uart_puts("process: result: ");
    uart_puts(value);
    uart_puts("\n");
    process_exit(0);
}
The local array has five bytes: B, V, S, 1, and the terminating zero byte. It is modifiable character data. The helper receives a pointer to that existing array; passing it does not create another array or another process.
The helper first exchanges positions 0 and 1, prints the intermediate value, calls process_yield(), then exchanges positions 2 and 3. Before yield, both worker() and permute() are still active. The array has become VBS1\0, but its owning worker invocation has not ended.

4.8 The process stack is an array in an object
Exercise 2 declares:
void main(void)
{
    struct process p;
    /* create, run, inspect state, run again */
    /* ... */
}
Here p is an automatic object in main()’s OS-stack allocation. The embedded array p.stack is part of that object. It reserves a subregion that becomes the process’s active call stack when sp selects an aligned position inside it.
This gives the process distinct stack storage for its execution without creating another ELF section. While the process runs, OS-side calls are suspended. When the OS regains control, it uses other stack space and leaves p.stack intact. main() remains active throughout, so the process object and its embedded storage remain alive.
If p were instead static/global, its containing storage would be static. The role of its stack array would remain process-owned runtime storage. Therefore “process stack” answers what the memory is used for; the declaration and allocation answer where that memory comes from. In this example no dynamic allocation is required.
